  "configuration_id": "config_033",
  "reason": "Evidence: All five completed experiments use singleprofile; the best result (config_046, acc=0.537, F1=0.518) uses multimodal with scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l and weighted fusion. RNA dominates (0.520 vs 0.227 for image-only), scvi_n200 beats pca_n2000 by ~3.5 pp, and weighted fusion slightly edges concat. Uncertainty: The profile setting (single vs. multiprofile) and aggregation strategy are completely unexplored—no experiment has tested whether multiple profiles plus an aggregation step can improve on the best single-profile result. Question: Does adding multiprofile with the simplest aggregation (avgpool) to our best encoder/fusion combination yield a meaningful gain over config_046? Why this is the best next experiment: config_033 changes exactly one design axis (singleprofile→multiprofile + avgpool) while holding the proven best encoders (scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l) and fusion (weighted) constant, giving a clean, interpretable comparison against config_046. If it helps, we can then explore transformer aggregation; if it does not, we efficiently rule out a large sub-space of multiprofile configurations and focus remaining budget on other axes (e.g., scgpt encoder, resnet50 image encoder)."
}

